13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues favoring A correctly note the sequential formation of hairpins, yet they continue to under-weight the physical constraint imposed by the ribosome under high tryptophan. When Trp is abundant the ribosome translates through region 1 and remains bound across region 2 up to the stop codon; any 1–2 mutation therefore cannot free region 2 for antiterminator pairing because the ribosome itself occludes that segment. Region 3 and 4 are transcribed while the ribosome is still in place, allowing the 3–4 terminator to assemble.  

By contrast, a mutation confined to region 2 directly alters the overlapping bases shared by both the 2–3 antiterminator and the 3–4 terminator. Even after the ribosome eventually advances and releases region 2, the altered sequence shifts the folding equilibrium so that region 3 cannot achieve the precise, stable pairing geometry required for a functional 3–4 stem-loop. The result is read-through specifically under repressing conditions—the exact phenotype asked.  

C only changes the downstream U-run that governs release timing after the hairpin has already formed and therefore leaves the upstream stem intact. The interdependence of the overlapping segments therefore continues to favor B over A as the mutation most consistent with continued transcription when tryptophan is high.}

