5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues favoring A continue to treat the 1-2 pause structure as the decisive early competitor whose removal automatically frees region 2 for antiterminator pairing, yet this still under-weights the physical reality of ribosome positioning under high tryptophan. When Trp is abundant the ribosome translates region 1 without stalling and remains sterically bound across region 2 up to the stop codon; therefore any mutation confined to region 1 cannot liberate region 2 for 2-3 pairing while the ribosome is in place. Regions 3 and 4 are transcribed while that occlusion persists, allowing 3-4 terminator formation to proceed.  

By contrast, a mutation limited to region 2 directly alters the overlapping bases that region 3 must later use whether it attempts 2-3 antiterminator or 3-4 terminator pairing. Even after the ribosome eventually advances and clears region 2, the altered sequence shifts the post-release folding equilibrium so that the precise, stable geometry required for a functional 3-4 stem-loop becomes less probable. This produces read-through specifically under repressing conditions—the phenotype the question asks for.  

C only modulates the downstream U-run that governs release timing after an already-formed hairpin and therefore leaves the stem itself intact; it cannot abolish terminator assembly. The interdependence of the overlapping segments, together with the ribosome’s physical masking under high Trp, therefore continues to make B the mutation most consistent with continued transcription when tryptophan is abundant.}

